    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • The Volkswagen Group's Affordable BEV Quartet Has Almost Reached 150,000 Orders\nThe Mazda CX-30's 2027 Update Expands UK Range From 17 to 21 Versions\n16 photos\nPhoto: Mazda\nOver in the United Kingdom, prospective customers now have more ways to spend money on the CX-30. Even though Mazda has not made any mechanical changes to the front-biased crossover, it did grow the UK range to a grand total of 21 versions for model year 2027.\nUnder the hood, buyers are presented with a choice between 140 or 186 metric ponies of e-Skyactiv G or e-Skyactiv X muscle. That's 138 and 183 mechanical horsepower, respectively, and only e-Skyactiv X specifications can be furthered with all-wheel drive. In this case, i-Activ stands for an electronically controlled system that comes in the form of a power transfer unit.\nSaid all-wheel-drive setup favors 2WD operation, leaving the front wheels do the talking for most of the time in order to save fuel. The rear coupling, meanwhile, does its job under hard acceleration, spirited cornering, and when front slip is detected. Mazda says the system can predict a traction shortfall, responding more aggressively if the front wheels do slip.\nOnly the base Prime Line trim level is available with either a manual or an automatic. Everything else comes with the six-speed automatic exclusively. Customers who prefer the more interesting route should go for the e-Skyactiv X, which switches between compression and spark ignition depending on load. The X and G families of engines will be ultimately discontinued by the Skyactiv Z family, whose party trick is a Lambda 1.0 air-fuel ratio across a wider load band.\nNew for 2027 in the United Kingdom, the Homura Plus grade slots above Homura and builds on said version's blacked-out exterior treatment. Additions include black leather upholstery, 12-speaker Bose audio, power-adjustable front seats, upgraded LED lighting, gunmetal and gloss-black interior trim, and a 360-degree camera system.\nPhoto: Mazda The headlight addition for 2027, however, is ventilation for the front seats. A CX-30 first, it does sound minor until you remember how much of this crossover segment is decided by how much equipment can be crammed in such a small package. Cooled front seats give Mazda a concrete answer to increasingly more competitive alternatives, and the Homura Plus further sweetens the deal with a heating function.\nHomura customers, on the other hand, are presented with a bit of a visual makeover. The beltline moldings, grille surround wing, roof rails, and all exterior badging go black, giving this grade a darker look that leans into the sporty lines of the CX-30. The Exclusive-Line and Takumi take the opposite route, boasting silver for their beltline trim pieces, grille surround wing, roof rails, and badging. Takumi remains the flagship, and it gains a 360-degree camera for model year 2027.\nSee-Through View mode allows the driver to see around obstacles ahead of the car while maneuvering, which should come in handy in a variety of scenarios. Think of it as an upgraded surround-view system designed to reveal hazards around the vehicle when parking or maneuvering in very tight places. Entry-level buyers haven't been forgotten either, for the Prime-Line gains enhanced Smart Brake Support and Blind Spot Monitoring.\nThe latter now includes a door-opening warning for approaching traffic. Driver Monitoring is now standard across the range, while Center-Like trims move from 16-inch gray alloy wheels to an 18-inch design in silver.\nAlongside Aero Gray, the color palette gains a pale green shade dubbed Zinc Green Metallic. The Makoto special edition will follow in early 2027, beautifying the CX-30 with a light cloth and stone-colored artificial leather interior. Dark-plated badging, a black front signature wing, black alloys, and black mirror caps complete the Makoto's visual theme.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nmazda cx-30 2027 Mazda CX-30 Mazda UK specifications\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT Apple Maps Gets DCE Update As There's Basically No Reason To Dump Google Maps",
